26
00:01:05,273 --> 00:01:06,882
(The generator of amazing songs)

27
00:01:06,883 --> 00:01:09,683
(Crying Nut's Bassist Han Kyung Rok)

28
00:01:10,253 --> 00:01:11,352
Nice to meet you.

29
00:01:11,353 --> 00:01:12,983
- Rock and roll.
- Rock and roll.

30
00:01:13,083 --> 00:01:14,382
- Kyung Rok.
- My gosh.

31
00:01:14,383 --> 00:01:16,152
- Say hello to our viewers.
- Sure.

32
00:01:16,153 --> 00:01:18,993
Hello.
It's the Year of the Red Horse.

33
00:01:19,193 --> 00:01:21,292
And no band fits the year
better than Crying Nut.

34
00:01:21,293 --> 00:01:22,622
My name is Han Kyung Rok.
Nice to meet you.

35
00:01:22,623 --> 00:01:24,792
(I'm Han Kyung Rok of Crying Nut.)

36
00:01:24,793 --> 00:01:26,032
Nice to meet you.

37
00:01:26,033 --> 00:01:27,902
Then were you also born
in the Year of the Horse?

38
00:01:27,903 --> 00:01:29,202
I wasn't born
in the Year of the Horse.

39
00:01:29,203 --> 00:01:31,672
I was born in 1977.

40
00:01:31,673 --> 00:01:32,703
Snake!

41
00:01:32,773 --> 00:01:35,243
- I'm the same age as you. Yes.
- Snake! Yes.

42
00:01:35,273 --> 00:01:36,913
- Come on. Yes.
- Come on. Yes.

43
00:01:37,313 --> 00:01:39,212
- I'm happy to meet a friend.
- Same here.

44
00:01:39,213 --> 00:01:40,282
- Oh, they're the same age?
- We've never met before.

45
00:01:40,283 --> 00:01:41,382
- Oh, yes.
- Right.

46
00:01:41,383 --> 00:01:42,383
- Have you been well?
- Yes.

47
00:01:42,384 --> 00:01:43,452
By the way, I was born
in the Year of the Snake too.

48
00:01:43,453 --> 00:01:44,712
- We have the same zodiac animal.
- Yes, another snake.

49
00:01:44,713 --> 00:01:45,883
- Snake.
- Yes.

50
00:01:46,253 --> 00:01:47,852
(Deep in the snakes)

51
00:01:47,853 --> 00:01:49,152
You can't do things like that here.

52
00:01:49,153 --> 00:01:50,822
Deep in the snakes

53
00:01:50,823 --> 00:01:52,222
You can't do things like that here.

54
00:01:52,223 --> 00:01:53,722
(Fascinated)

55
00:01:53,723 --> 00:01:55,123
"Deep in the snakes."

56
00:01:55,363 --> 00:01:56,492
That line doesn't make sense.

57
00:01:56,493 --> 00:01:57,493
Right.

58
00:01:57,494 --> 00:01:58,832
- Don't say nonsensical things.
- But...

59
00:01:58,833 --> 00:02:00,002
- I like that.
- I'll only say logical things.

60
00:02:00,003 --> 00:02:01,332
- Let's begin our new project,
- Yes.

61
00:02:01,333 --> 00:02:02,632
- "Running Horses."
- I'm...

62
00:02:02,633 --> 00:02:04,103
I'm still on that line,
"deep in the snakes."

63
00:02:04,403 --> 00:02:05,773
(Deeply affected)

64
00:02:06,573 --> 00:02:07,672
- That was legendary.
- Listen.

65
00:02:07,673 --> 00:02:10,072
(If you want,
I'll give you an encore.)

66
00:02:10,073 --> 00:02:11,173
- Hey, that's...
- Yes!

67
00:02:11,473 --> 00:02:12,473
(The audience is satisfied.)

68
00:02:12,474 --> 00:02:14,382
It looks like
he's struggling to blend in here.

69
00:02:14,383 --> 00:02:15,912
The second star of Running Horses...

70
00:02:15,913 --> 00:02:19,683
is Han Kyung Rok. Let's take a look
at his Rainbow Live.

71
00:02:20,683 --> 00:02:21,723
Rainbow.

72
00:02:22,783 --> 00:02:25,052
Let's run together.
Rock and roll, come on!

73
00:02:25,053 --> 00:02:28,492
(Let's run together.
Rock and roll, come on!)

74
00:02:28,493 --> 00:02:29,563
I love it.

75
00:02:30,763 --> 00:02:32,233
I can't get used to his style.

76
00:02:32,563 --> 00:02:34,933
It looks like
I'm rocking the opening segment.

77
00:02:35,333 --> 00:02:36,363
My gosh.

78
00:02:36,563 --> 00:02:38,803
(The Year of the Red Horse)

79
00:02:43,643 --> 00:02:46,373
(Rainbow Running Horses)

80
00:02:47,713 --> 00:02:49,312
- "Captain Rock."
- "Captain Rock."

81
00:02:49,313 --> 00:02:50,513
- Rock.
- Rock.

82
00:02:51,883 --> 00:02:53,923
(Kyung Rok is sleeping,
covered in the blanket.)

83
00:02:55,283 --> 00:02:57,293
(Swinging)

84
00:02:59,693 --> 00:03:01,193
(He gives the blanket a good kick.)

85
00:03:01,763 --> 00:03:04,163
(Groaning)

86
00:03:05,593 --> 00:03:07,932
(Banging his head in his sleep)

87
00:03:07,933 --> 00:03:09,363
- He's adorable.
- My gosh.

88
00:03:10,973 --> 00:03:13,332
(Stretching)

89
00:03:13,333 --> 00:03:15,173
- Oh, my. He's flexible.
- He's super flexible.

90
00:03:15,903 --> 00:03:18,212
I've been holding an instrument
for 30 years.

91
00:03:18,213 --> 00:03:20,013
- So my back is all out of shape.
- I bet.

92
00:03:20,743 --> 00:03:23,113
So I've been taking Pilates classes
for two years.

93
00:03:24,313 --> 00:03:27,053
(Grabbing)

94
00:03:29,483 --> 00:03:30,623
- They're smart curtains.
- Smart curtains.

95
00:03:30,853 --> 00:03:32,022
They're motorized curtains.

96
00:03:32,023 --> 00:03:33,093
(Bright sunlight falls
on the dark rocker's face.)

97
00:03:33,723 --> 00:03:35,323
(Rubbing his face)

98
00:03:36,593 --> 00:03:37,893
He has a thick head of hair.

99
00:03:39,363 --> 00:03:42,463
Hello. I've been living alone
for 20 years.

100
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:44,632
I'm a bassist of Crying Nut.
My name is Han Kyung Rok.

101
00:03:44,633 --> 00:03:45,633
Nice to meet you.

102
00:03:47,803 --> 00:03:52,642
(Crying Nut wrote timeless classics
recognized by everyone.)

103
00:03:52,643 --> 00:03:55,042
(Source: Crying Nut)

104
00:03:55,043 --> 00:04:00,013
(And this is where it all began.)

105
00:04:01,523 --> 00:04:04,122
(In 1993,)

106
00:04:04,123 --> 00:04:05,793
(Crying Nut was formed.)

107
00:04:06,293 --> 00:04:08,592
(In 2001, Incheon
International Airport opened...)

108
00:04:08,593 --> 00:04:10,933
(and Crying Nut released
"Deep in the Night.")

109
00:04:11,233 --> 00:04:12,862
(Source: Ssamzi Sound Festival)

110
00:04:12,863 --> 00:04:18,132
(Source: Crying Nut)

111
00:04:18,133 --> 00:04:19,503
(From the underground scene
to the legendary band)

112
00:04:19,933 --> 00:04:24,813
(The history of indie rock bands)

113
00:04:25,473 --> 00:04:30,813
(Bassist of Crying Nut,
Han Kyung Rok)

114
00:04:32,113 --> 00:04:33,853
They made their debut during
the Kim Young Sam presidency.

115
00:04:33,883 --> 00:04:35,883
I was born in 1997. So that's...

116
00:04:36,623 --> 00:04:38,523
- It was before you were born.
- Yes, that's right.

117
00:04:39,193 --> 00:04:41,293
When you were born,
he had to face the economic crisis.

118
00:04:41,563 --> 00:04:43,993
Right. The economic crisis
took place in 1997.

119
00:04:44,063 --> 00:04:45,162
- It was really tough.
- How many presidents...

120
00:04:45,163 --> 00:04:46,193
have l lived through?

121
00:04:46,863 --> 00:04:49,363
(The legendary band, Crying Nut,
was formed 33 years ago.)

122
00:04:50,203 --> 00:04:53,403
(The current time is 9:05am.)

123
00:04:54,403 --> 00:04:56,673
(Getting up)

124
00:04:57,213 --> 00:04:58,373
He got up in a cool way.

125
00:05:00,283 --> 00:05:04,013
(Gosh, it's chilly.)

126
00:05:05,053 --> 00:05:08,683
(He grabs the drying rack
as soon as he gets up.)

127
00:05:10,393 --> 00:05:11,692
All right.

128
00:05:11,693 --> 00:05:13,723
(Setting it up next to his bed)

129
00:05:14,463 --> 00:05:15,662
All right. My gosh.

130
00:05:15,663 --> 00:05:17,433
- Why put the blanket on the rack?
- What's going on?

131
00:05:17,663 --> 00:05:20,363
I sweat a lot during the night.

132
00:05:20,503 --> 00:05:22,633
It's too humid.
The blanket gets wet.

133
00:05:22,933 --> 00:05:24,732
- You're drying it.
- You disinfect it in the sun.

134
00:05:24,733 --> 00:05:25,833
Yes. I'm disinfecting it.

135
00:05:26,503 --> 00:05:29,643
(He sun-dries the blanket
as soon as he wakes up.)

136
00:05:30,573 --> 00:05:33,173
(Looking disheveled)

137
00:05:36,283 --> 00:05:37,313
Gosh, his house...

138
00:05:38,253 --> 00:05:40,253
(Past the foyer,)

139
00:05:40,353 --> 00:05:42,923
(there's a fancy living room.)

140
00:05:44,553 --> 00:05:48,693
I thought you'd live
in a semi-basement unit in Hongdae.

141
00:05:49,863 --> 00:05:51,093
I used to have
the rock spirit before.

142
00:05:51,263 --> 00:05:53,933
He's a rocker. So I assumed
there would be broken bottles.

143
00:05:54,463 --> 00:05:56,533
- A lot of people assume that.
- Yes.

144
00:05:57,003 --> 00:05:58,873
I thought there would be
punch marks on the walls too.

145
00:06:00,003 --> 00:06:02,143
(A clean interior,
free of any punch marks)

146
00:06:02,643 --> 00:06:05,443
(Chairs in different colors)

147
00:06:06,013 --> 00:06:07,542
- That's cute.
- It's so cute.

148
00:06:07,543 --> 00:06:08,582
(Decor items arranged
to his personal taste)

149
00:06:08,583 --> 00:06:10,043
This shows why
personal tastes are so important.

150
00:06:13,383 --> 00:06:14,752
I thought there would be writing...

151
00:06:14,753 --> 00:06:16,682
- on the walls in paint.
- Like graffiti?

152
00:06:16,683 --> 00:06:19,022
It's so clean and neat
for a rocker's house.

153
00:06:19,023 --> 00:06:20,123
- Right.
- Right?

154
00:06:20,923 --> 00:06:23,523
(Thinking)

155
00:06:24,793 --> 00:06:26,392
I don't see any rebellious spirit
in your house.

156
00:06:26,393 --> 00:06:27,433
Right.

157
00:06:27,603 --> 00:06:29,403
- I take care of that when I'm out.
- Outside?

158
00:06:30,433 --> 00:06:32,272
Your house is covered
with capitalism.

159
00:06:32,273 --> 00:06:35,103
- Do you know what's fascinating?
- Yes?

160
00:06:35,703 --> 00:06:38,042
The sentiment from Hongdae
still remains in his house.

161
00:06:38,043 --> 00:06:39,113
- Where?
- Right.

162
00:06:39,243 --> 00:06:40,943
Look at his lamp.

163
00:06:41,383 --> 00:06:43,413
It's the way he arranged
the furniture in his house.

164
00:06:43,543 --> 00:06:45,782
It reminds me of...

165
00:06:45,783 --> 00:06:47,683
a successful musician's studio
in Hongdae.

166
00:06:49,523 --> 00:06:51,322
(His personal taste is reflected
in the interior design.)

167
00:06:51,323 --> 00:06:52,693
His house is so colorful.

168
00:06:52,853 --> 00:06:53,993
I like this vibe.

169
00:06:55,293 --> 00:06:57,732
(Plants are seen
throughout his house.)

170
00:06:57,733 --> 00:06:59,892
It's so different from the house
I had in mind.

171
00:06:59,893 --> 00:07:01,663
- Same here. I'm quite surprised.
- My gosh.

172
00:07:02,833 --> 00:07:05,633
I realized something
after moving into this house.

173
00:07:06,073 --> 00:07:09,043
Over 50 percent of interior design
goes into lighting.

174
00:07:10,143 --> 00:07:11,843
Lighting sets the overall vibe.

175
00:07:12,913 --> 00:07:15,943
(His house is unusually filled
with light sources.)

176
00:07:16,443 --> 00:07:18,752
The stained glass lamp
is so beautiful. It's this one.

177
00:07:18,753 --> 00:07:20,113
- Oh, this one.
- It's got a nice vibe.

178
00:07:21,223 --> 00:07:24,093
(The lamps brighten
the atmosphere of his house.)

179
00:07:26,653 --> 00:07:27,723
Is this a church?

180
00:07:28,293 --> 00:07:29,293
- A church?
- Yes.

181
00:07:29,294 --> 00:07:30,663
I wanted the church
and cathedral vibe.

182
00:07:30,833 --> 00:07:33,462
- We see that at cathedrals.
- When colorful lights pour...

183
00:07:33,463 --> 00:07:36,163
into the house, it sets a holy vibe.

184
00:07:37,403 --> 00:07:39,503
Lighting is very important to me.

185
00:07:40,273 --> 00:07:42,443
When I gaze at someplace bright,

186
00:07:43,543 --> 00:07:46,473
I get this sense of warmth
for some reason.

187
00:07:47,313 --> 00:07:49,613
It comforts me
in a gentle and soft way.

188
00:07:50,413 --> 00:07:53,483
My house now faces southeast.

189
00:07:54,053 --> 00:07:55,723
That's why it's very well-lit.

190
00:07:56,283 --> 00:08:00,493
I lived in Hongdae for 20 years.

191
00:08:00,863 --> 00:08:03,363
Actually, Hongdae...

192
00:08:03,863 --> 00:08:05,732
is my turf.

193
00:08:05,733 --> 00:08:07,802
So I have a lot of friends...

194
00:08:07,803 --> 00:08:09,763
and shops in Hongdae.

195
00:08:10,433 --> 00:08:13,032
So when I stepped out of my house,
it was like being in Las Vegas...

196
00:08:13,033 --> 00:08:15,342
or at an amusement park.
I had so much fun every day.

197
00:08:15,343 --> 00:08:16,473
Every day was a party.

198
00:08:17,073 --> 00:08:19,373
So when I thought about it,

199
00:08:19,713 --> 00:08:22,043
I had been performing for 30 years.

200
00:08:22,313 --> 00:08:24,613
But for the first time in my career,
I left Hongdae.

201
00:08:24,853 --> 00:08:28,053
Then I moved to Goyang.
I guess you can sort of say...

202
00:08:28,153 --> 00:08:30,693
I exiled myself here.

203
00:08:31,123 --> 00:08:32,853
So I moved to a different area.

204
00:08:33,823 --> 00:08:36,523
I've been living here
for about seven months now.

205
00:08:37,193 --> 00:08:38,662
I think I lived in Hongdae...

206
00:08:38,663 --> 00:08:40,133
- longer than in my hometown.
- Yes, you did.

207
00:08:40,333 --> 00:08:41,363
Where did you grow up?

208
00:08:41,503 --> 00:08:43,733
- Dongbuichon-dong, Seoul.
- Dongbuichon-dong?

209
00:08:44,133 --> 00:08:46,672
- You lived in a fancy neighborhood.
- It doesn't suit you at all.

210
00:08:46,673 --> 00:08:47,942
It's different from a rock spirit.

211
00:08:47,943 --> 00:08:50,303
- No, there was a spirit back then.
- Really?

212
00:08:51,343 --> 00:08:53,012
I started off my career
in Hongdae too.

213
00:08:53,013 --> 00:08:54,082
- Really?
- Yes.

214
00:08:54,083 --> 00:08:55,543
My place was
around Yeonnam Police Box.

215
00:08:55,683 --> 00:08:56,913
- Around there?
- Same here.

216
00:08:57,053 --> 00:08:58,452
I lived in a semi-basement unit...

217
00:08:58,453 --> 00:08:59,853
- in Sangsu-dong with Mal Nyeon.
- What?

218
00:09:00,313 --> 00:09:01,682
I'm also from Sangsu.

219
00:09:01,683 --> 00:09:03,352
- For some reason, I thought...
- I bet we brushed past each other.

220
00:09:03,353 --> 00:09:06,023
- I must live in Hongdae to do art.
- Hongdae.

221
00:09:06,653 --> 00:09:08,823
(Trudging)

222
00:09:09,463 --> 00:09:10,562
(He puts on music
as soon as he wakes up.)

223
00:09:10,563 --> 00:09:11,633
It's an LP record.

224
00:09:12,333 --> 00:09:13,393
I wonder what kind of music
he'll listen to.

225
00:09:15,103 --> 00:09:16,162
What?

226
00:09:16,163 --> 00:09:17,302
(The rocker listens to
classical music in the morning.)

227
00:09:17,303 --> 00:09:18,373
It's by Bach.

228
00:09:18,973 --> 00:09:20,103
It's a completely different vibe.

229
00:09:21,073 --> 00:09:24,513
I like listening to classical music
in the morning.

230
00:09:25,143 --> 00:09:27,713
Hi, Rock Pup. Good morning.

231
00:09:28,213 --> 00:09:29,283
Did you sleep well?

232
00:09:29,913 --> 00:09:31,182
(Rock Pup, a pet doll)

233
00:09:31,183 --> 00:09:32,783
- The one from IKEA.
- That's right.

234
00:09:33,483 --> 00:09:35,953
(Turning on)

235
00:09:36,353 --> 00:09:38,193
Gosh. You must be excited.

236
00:09:38,853 --> 00:09:39,923
Let's eat.

237
00:09:40,193 --> 00:09:41,362
The fish look cute.

238
00:09:41,363 --> 00:09:42,393
(A morning greeting
to the school of his pet fish)

239
00:09:42,823 --> 00:09:43,962
How long have you had them?

240
00:09:43,963 --> 00:09:45,963
It's been about two years.

241
00:09:46,963 --> 00:09:50,363
Here comes the tasty meat.

242
00:09:51,173 --> 00:09:52,503
They're having a blast.

243
00:09:53,733 --> 00:09:56,403
(After feeding the fish...)

244
00:09:56,943 --> 00:09:58,872
(Grabbing)

245
00:09:58,873 --> 00:09:59,943
What's that?

246
00:10:00,643 --> 00:10:02,183
(Smelling)

247
00:10:02,743 --> 00:10:03,953
It smells great.

248
00:10:04,313 --> 00:10:06,013
Musicians love flowers.

249
00:10:06,183 --> 00:10:07,452
- Right.
- Most of us do.

250
00:10:07,453 --> 00:10:08,483
(Heading to the kitchen
while carrying the vases)

251
00:10:09,383 --> 00:10:12,322
(The rocker starts his morning
with flowers.)

252
00:10:12,323 --> 00:10:15,123
I've been growing flowers
for 20 years.

253
00:10:15,723 --> 00:10:17,633
I had always liked flowers
ever since I was a kid.

254
00:10:17,963 --> 00:10:19,733
I bought flowers
for my friends for no reason...

255
00:10:19,863 --> 00:10:22,003
while walking on the street.

256
00:10:22,533 --> 00:10:24,032
I do that. I like flowers.

257
00:10:24,033 --> 00:10:25,102
That's romantic.

258
00:10:25,103 --> 00:10:26,273
Flowers are romantic.

259
00:10:28,173 --> 00:10:29,672
(Smiling)

260
00:10:29,673 --> 00:10:31,013
Even the vases look beautiful.

261
00:10:31,613 --> 00:10:34,342
I change the water in the vases
every morning.

262
00:10:34,343 --> 00:10:35,513
I did it this morning too.

263
00:10:36,483 --> 00:10:37,782
One must be diligent...

264
00:10:37,783 --> 00:10:39,082
- to grow plants and flowers.
- Right.

265
00:10:39,083 --> 00:10:40,153
- Right.
- I'm impressed.

266
00:10:40,853 --> 00:10:42,923
(After taking care of
his fish and flowers,)

267
00:10:43,623 --> 00:10:45,453
(he lies down in the living room.)

268
00:10:50,893 --> 00:10:53,163
I have a daily routine.

269
00:10:53,963 --> 00:10:55,102
You're on a foam roller.

270
00:10:55,103 --> 00:10:57,133
Yes. I love stretching
on foam rollers.

271
00:10:59,033 --> 00:11:00,973
- You're quite flexible.
- Yes.

272
00:11:02,503 --> 00:11:04,913
(Rolling)

273
00:11:06,773 --> 00:11:08,642
He has two foam rollers.
It's like watching a circus.

274
00:11:08,643 --> 00:11:10,043
(Laughing)

275
00:11:11,313 --> 00:11:14,183
That's the most well-lit spot...

276
00:11:14,953 --> 00:11:16,652
in my house.

277
00:11:16,653 --> 00:11:18,722
It feels
as if the sunlight cleansed my soul.

278
00:11:18,723 --> 00:11:20,422
I can feel my soul getting cleansed.

279
00:11:20,423 --> 00:11:21,493
It's a routine for actresses.

280
00:11:22,263 --> 00:11:23,493
What's up with your daily routine?

281
00:11:24,193 --> 00:11:25,632
He's so different from rockers...

282
00:11:25,633 --> 00:11:27,663
- we had in mind.
- Right? The genres have changed.

283
00:11:28,833 --> 00:11:32,473
(His rock spirit is still sleeping
in this gentle morning.)

284
00:11:34,173 --> 00:11:36,703
(Walking his hands back)

285
00:11:38,843 --> 00:11:41,843
(Feeling refreshed)

286
00:11:42,243 --> 00:11:45,213
(After his morning stretch,)

287
00:11:46,013 --> 00:11:49,453
(he washes his face
with the headband on.)

288
00:11:50,723 --> 00:11:52,823
- That's pretty powerful.
- He washes his face like an oldie.

289
00:11:53,293 --> 00:11:54,422
He washed behind his ears too.

290
00:11:54,423 --> 00:11:55,563
Right.

291
00:11:56,523 --> 00:11:57,632
I feel so fresh.

292
00:11:57,633 --> 00:11:59,533
(I feel so fresh.)

293
00:12:01,533 --> 00:12:04,333
(Slapping)

294
00:12:05,433 --> 00:12:07,573
- But doesn't that feel refreshing?
- Right.

295
00:12:07,673 --> 00:12:09,172
- I feel like I must slap hard...
- Yes.

296
00:12:09,173 --> 00:12:11,413
- for my skin to absorb the toner.
- Everyone does that.

297
00:12:13,443 --> 00:12:14,743
My gosh.

298
00:12:15,843 --> 00:12:17,483
It's over.

299
00:12:18,353 --> 00:12:19,852
There must always be music.

300
00:12:19,853 --> 00:12:21,652
(Captain Rock can't live
without music.)

301
00:12:21,653 --> 00:12:22,923
It's "La Bamba."

302
00:12:24,123 --> 00:12:26,893
(Humming)

303
00:12:28,193 --> 00:12:31,292
(He turns on the lamp
while dancing.)

304
00:12:31,293 --> 00:12:32,763
(Beeping)

305
00:12:33,463 --> 00:12:36,663
(Sliding open)

306
00:12:38,403 --> 00:12:41,943
(Heading to the kitchen
after securing the sunlight)

307
00:12:42,303 --> 00:12:44,372
Your morning is really well-spent.

308
00:12:44,373 --> 00:12:46,172
Yes. I'm a morning person.

309
00:12:46,173 --> 00:12:48,443
- Really?
- I love spending my morning.

310
00:12:48,683 --> 00:12:49,983
I thought he'd be a night owl.

311
00:12:52,183 --> 00:12:54,923
(Having a blast in the morning)

312
00:12:55,953 --> 00:12:58,493
(The sentimental rocker's morning
routine flows without interruption.)

313
00:12:59,123 --> 00:13:00,323
Play the guitar, Rock Pup!

314
00:13:03,823 --> 00:13:05,393
He's like the main character
in an American TV show.

315
00:13:07,063 --> 00:13:09,462
I bet you toss trash
like you're playing basketball.

316
00:13:09,463 --> 00:13:10,533
I bet you do that.

317
00:13:10,903 --> 00:13:12,533
He also reminds me of Kevin
in "Home Alone."

318
00:13:13,173 --> 00:13:14,173
When his mom isn't home.

319
00:13:14,643 --> 00:13:15,673
(Whirring)

320
00:13:16,503 --> 00:13:17,512
(Drip coffee)

321
00:13:17,513 --> 00:13:18,713
You're making coffee?

322
00:13:19,413 --> 00:13:22,583
I didn't learn it properly
at an academy or anything.

323
00:13:23,213 --> 00:13:25,713
I've been making my own drip coffee
for over ten years.

324
00:13:26,953 --> 00:13:28,483
Then he must be a real expert.

325
00:13:28,523 --> 00:13:30,253
- Yes.
- He's been doing it for a decade.

326
00:13:30,823 --> 00:13:32,253
I enjoy those coffee-making moments.

327
00:13:33,153 --> 00:13:35,392
It feels like I'm spending
those moments only for myself.

328
00:13:35,393 --> 00:13:37,262
There's no right answer in flavors.

329
00:13:37,263 --> 00:13:39,962
I can change my coffee
based on my mood that day.

330
00:13:39,963 --> 00:13:41,102
It's fun.

331
00:13:41,103 --> 00:13:44,102
I'll give you
a coffee-related present next time.

332
00:13:44,103 --> 00:13:45,203
Really?

333
00:13:46,173 --> 00:13:48,503
(Thinking)

334
00:13:48,803 --> 00:13:52,012
(He thinks for a moment
and then grabs something.)

335
00:13:52,013 --> 00:13:54,182
The floral tea set is...

336
00:13:54,183 --> 00:13:56,143
from my mother's house.

337
00:13:56,443 --> 00:13:58,382
But it looks more stylish
because it's at your place.

338
00:13:58,383 --> 00:14:00,513
- Right.
- It doesn't look like hers.

339
00:14:02,183 --> 00:14:03,283
Coffee time.

340
00:14:05,023 --> 00:14:06,622
My gosh.

341
00:14:06,623 --> 00:14:10,363
(He sits at the table where
he turned on the light before.)

342
00:14:11,693 --> 00:14:12,892
He looks so classy.

343
00:14:12,893 --> 00:14:15,702
(Captain Rock has
quite the classy taste.)

344
00:14:15,703 --> 00:14:17,203
That's a nice morning routine.

345
00:14:19,803 --> 00:14:22,543
(Sipping)

346
00:14:22,843 --> 00:14:24,973
(Hitting the spot)

347
00:14:31,983 --> 00:14:34,353
(This is nice.)

348
00:14:34,953 --> 00:14:38,792
(Enjoying a moment
while gazing into the sunlight)

349
00:14:38,793 --> 00:14:40,523
Everything is so different,
but harmonizes so well.

350
00:14:40,593 --> 00:14:43,522
Right. Without a good taste,
it would've looked so chaotic.

351
00:14:43,523 --> 00:14:45,593
- Right.
- There's no sense of unity.

352
00:14:45,893 --> 00:14:47,532
But strangely,
they blend in well together.

353
00:14:47,533 --> 00:14:48,533
(A collage of his layered tastes)

354
00:14:50,163 --> 00:14:53,903
(Let me see.)

355
00:14:56,973 --> 00:14:58,013
What?

356
00:14:58,343 --> 00:14:59,373
That's not a bass guitar.

357
00:15:00,673 --> 00:15:02,483
I play the guitar too.

358
00:15:02,643 --> 00:15:04,383
I write a lot of songs
with my acoustic guitar.

359
00:15:06,183 --> 00:15:07,213
He's so cool.

360
00:15:12,323 --> 00:15:13,893
(Humming)

361
00:15:15,363 --> 00:15:17,763
(Pondering)

362
00:15:18,433 --> 00:15:23,163
(He types something on his laptop
while mumbling something.)

363
00:15:24,103 --> 00:15:25,173
"Send..."

364
00:15:25,633 --> 00:15:27,503
- My gosh.
- He's writing the lyrics.

365
00:15:28,973 --> 00:15:31,112
"I..."

366
00:15:31,113 --> 00:15:33,972
(While basking in the warm sun,)

367
00:15:33,973 --> 00:15:37,853
(he focuses on writing his song.)

368
00:15:39,783 --> 00:15:41,183
"It's okay."

369
00:15:41,423 --> 00:15:44,153
We're on different paths,
but in the same world

370
00:15:44,523 --> 00:15:46,622
I'm definitely a morning person...

371
00:15:46,623 --> 00:15:48,462
which isn't so common among rockers.

372
00:15:48,463 --> 00:15:51,193
Here's why. I also write a column.

373
00:15:51,433 --> 00:15:52,593
I'm a writer.

374
00:15:52,863 --> 00:15:54,903
On top of that,
I also write lyrics and melodies.

375
00:15:55,603 --> 00:15:56,932
There's something...

376
00:15:56,933 --> 00:15:58,472
different about my writing
when bathed in the morning sun.

377
00:15:58,473 --> 00:16:00,003
It's touched
by the morning moisture.

378
00:16:00,373 --> 00:16:01,743
- What a lovely expression.
- Like the morning dew.

379
00:16:02,543 --> 00:16:04,072
We're also part of nature.

380
00:16:04,073 --> 00:16:06,772
The morning makes me feel like
I'm budding like plants.

381
00:16:06,773 --> 00:16:07,983
My thoughts bloom
in the morning too.

382
00:16:08,083 --> 00:16:10,013
So I try to embrace
this sense of light...

383
00:16:10,383 --> 00:16:11,983
when I write my songs.

384
00:16:12,153 --> 00:16:13,513
His expressions are quite poetic.

385
00:16:14,083 --> 00:16:16,153
The most famous song
of Crying Nut is...

386
00:16:16,383 --> 00:16:17,653
"Luxembourg."

387
00:16:20,793 --> 00:16:23,463
("Luxembourg,"
lyrics and melody by Han Kyung Rok)

388
00:16:23,663 --> 00:16:25,892
(The initial draft
of Kyung Rok's lyrics)

389
00:16:25,893 --> 00:16:27,563
You have a notebook
with your lyrics.

390
00:16:28,263 --> 00:16:29,403
"Deep in the Night."

391
00:16:30,133 --> 00:16:31,202
"Deep in the Night."

392
00:16:31,203 --> 00:16:35,573
(He also wrote the lyrics
and melody of "Deep in the Night.")

393
00:16:35,743 --> 00:16:37,342
I've written...

394
00:16:37,343 --> 00:16:40,443
a lot of lyrics
for other singers as well.

395
00:16:40,613 --> 00:16:41,613
I wrote the lyrics...

396
00:16:41,614 --> 00:16:42,812
for "Sweet Little Kitty"
by Cherry Filter.

397
00:16:42,813 --> 00:16:45,283
- "Sweet Little Kitty" too?
- It was a huge hit.

398
00:16:45,483 --> 00:16:46,513
No way.

399
00:16:46,753 --> 00:16:48,523
- My gosh.
- It was a mega hit song.

400
00:16:48,983 --> 00:16:51,452
No way. He still has notes
for the song. That's crazy.

401
00:16:51,453 --> 00:16:52,592
That was the initial draft.

402
00:16:52,593 --> 00:16:54,663
"Small dancing lights."
You didn't change the lyrics.

403
00:16:55,093 --> 00:16:57,263
"Deep in the Night"
and "Sweet Little Kitty" used to...

404
00:16:57,533 --> 00:16:59,563
bring in a lot of royalties.

405
00:16:59,733 --> 00:17:02,162
Then Car the garden sang...

406
00:17:02,163 --> 00:17:03,303
"Myeongdong Calling."

407
00:17:03,733 --> 00:17:05,303
- "Myeongdong Calling."
- "Myeongdong Calling."

408
00:17:06,233 --> 00:17:08,573
(Car the garden's
sorrowful version...)

409
00:17:08,973 --> 00:17:10,943
(of "Myeongdong Calling"
was beloved by the listeners.)

410
00:17:11,613 --> 00:17:12,772
You're the original singer.

411
00:17:12,773 --> 00:17:14,212
(But the original song belongs
to Crying Nut.)

412
00:17:14,213 --> 00:17:15,213
No way.

413
00:17:15,313 --> 00:17:19,182
A long time ago, I had
a part-time job in Myeong-dong.

414
00:17:19,183 --> 00:17:20,652
- It was around Christmas.
- Myeong-dong.

415
00:17:20,653 --> 00:17:23,283
It was snowing and cold.

416
00:17:23,323 --> 00:17:25,393
But the couples looked so happy.

417
00:17:25,793 --> 00:17:29,523
I wrote "Myeongdong Calling"
based on that experience.

418
00:17:29,723 --> 00:17:31,432
- My gosh.
- From my memory of that day.

419
00:17:31,433 --> 00:17:34,463
You're inspired
by every moment of your life.

420
00:17:35,003 --> 00:17:36,632
(It looks like...)

421
00:17:36,633 --> 00:17:40,403
(his daily life and inspiration
go hand in hand.)

422
00:17:40,803 --> 00:17:42,872
- Rock and roll.
- He ends with "rock and roll."

423
00:17:42,873 --> 00:17:44,003
Peace.

424
00:17:44,443 --> 00:17:45,443
Hip-hop.

425
00:17:45,643 --> 00:17:47,243
(Finding him cute)

426
00:17:47,743 --> 00:17:51,313
(He finishes writing songs
in the morning on a good note.)

427
00:17:51,853 --> 00:17:53,883
I ought to eat something.

428
00:17:54,723 --> 00:17:55,983
(Opening his fridge)

429
00:17:56,783 --> 00:17:57,823
Okay.

430
00:17:59,493 --> 00:18:01,763
(Kimchi and delivery food
are in the fridge.)

431
00:18:02,263 --> 00:18:04,063
(A simple fridge)

432
00:18:04,293 --> 00:18:05,633
This is porridge.

433
00:18:07,163 --> 00:18:10,603
(Grabbing his old delivery food)

434
00:18:11,703 --> 00:18:14,703
(He also takes out
simple side dishes for the meal.)

435
00:18:15,443 --> 00:18:17,973
(He's going into the kitchen?)

436
00:18:18,813 --> 00:18:19,913
(Opening)

437
00:18:22,283 --> 00:18:23,443
Those cups look so cute.

438
00:18:24,483 --> 00:18:28,683
(Among many bowls,
he grabs the one he likes.)

439
00:18:30,453 --> 00:18:33,423
(Transferring a desired amount
of porridge to the bowl)

440
00:18:34,523 --> 00:18:35,822
(He opens up
the adjacent cabinet next.)

441
00:18:35,823 --> 00:18:37,733
(Thinking)

442
00:18:41,733 --> 00:18:43,563
You are moving them
on your own plate.

443
00:18:43,633 --> 00:18:45,933
- Yes, I got the food delivered.
- Okay.

444
00:18:46,133 --> 00:18:47,702
- Eating it in a pretty bowl...
- Right. I'd plate it too.

445
00:18:47,703 --> 00:18:49,103
and plating it nicely
before I eat it...

446
00:18:49,443 --> 00:18:51,972
make me feel like I'm loving myself.

447
00:18:51,973 --> 00:18:53,643
- Like I'm cherishing myself.
- Right.

448
00:18:53,673 --> 00:18:55,182
- This is nothing like...
- So...

449
00:18:55,183 --> 00:18:56,613
what Kian84 expected to see.

450
00:18:56,683 --> 00:18:59,282
- His house is so clean.
- This is great. I love it.

451
00:18:59,283 --> 00:19:01,153
- I'm sorry, I'm too clean.
- Gosh, no.

452
00:19:01,353 --> 00:19:03,253
- You're apologizing for that?
- You are...

453
00:19:03,953 --> 00:19:05,292
(Plating a bit of the salad)

454
00:19:05,293 --> 00:19:06,823
I get several dishes delivered.

455
00:19:07,223 --> 00:19:09,122
Then I come up with
my own combination for my meals.

456
00:19:09,123 --> 00:19:10,822
(He can't forget
about the porridge...)

457
00:19:10,823 --> 00:19:12,133
(and the toast.)

458
00:19:12,263 --> 00:19:14,602
- Kaya toast.
- Yes, it is.

459
00:19:14,603 --> 00:19:17,103
(How did these dishes
end up together?)

460
00:19:18,103 --> 00:19:19,272
It's like you're at a buffet.

461
00:19:19,273 --> 00:19:20,533
(A shocking combination
of Kyung Rok's meal)

462
00:19:21,073 --> 00:19:22,373
(Mixing)

463
00:19:23,443 --> 00:19:24,513
(Blowing)

464
00:19:25,043 --> 00:19:26,642
(He has a bite of the Korean dish,
the porridge, first.)

465
00:19:26,643 --> 00:19:27,643
(It's hot.)

466
00:19:31,253 --> 00:19:36,353
(Then he goes
after the Western-style salad.)

467
00:19:37,283 --> 00:19:39,923
(Munching)

468
00:19:41,293 --> 00:19:43,822
(Drinking the dongchimi juice
to finish his meal)

469
00:19:43,823 --> 00:19:45,593
(Hitting the spot)

470
00:19:52,033 --> 00:19:55,773
(I'm done with my meal.)

471
00:19:57,103 --> 00:19:58,813
(Water running)

472
00:19:59,413 --> 00:20:03,283
(He cleans up
right after his delicious meal.)

473
00:20:04,613 --> 00:20:06,813
(Clearing his throat)

474
00:20:13,753 --> 00:20:16,923
(Now that he's full,
he blasts the song he likes.)

475
00:20:17,423 --> 00:20:18,692
(Dancing to the song, "60's Cardin")

476
00:20:18,693 --> 00:20:21,032
There's a soundtrack
for everything you do.

477
00:20:21,033 --> 00:20:22,033
There is.

478
00:20:22,063 --> 00:20:24,033
He turned his life into a movie.

479
00:20:24,303 --> 00:20:27,033
I'd like to be the main character
of my life if possible.

480
00:20:27,073 --> 00:20:29,303
(Slicking it back)

481
00:20:30,343 --> 00:20:31,743
- My gosh.
- Oh, my.

482
00:20:34,443 --> 00:20:38,113
(Here comes Rocker Han Kyung Rok.)

483
00:20:39,583 --> 00:20:41,883
You have several bandanas
in the same design.

484
00:20:43,053 --> 00:20:44,423
I probably have 100 of them.

485
00:20:44,993 --> 00:20:47,493
I probably have 100 bandanas.
I have them in different colors too.

486
00:20:47,693 --> 00:20:48,922
(Wincing)

487
00:20:48,923 --> 00:20:49,923
It's his signature style.

488
00:20:51,533 --> 00:20:53,333
The moment I put on my bandana,

489
00:20:53,533 --> 00:20:54,663
I become Captain Rock.

490
00:20:54,833 --> 00:20:57,363
Should I say
it symbolizes freedom and romance?

491
00:21:02,273 --> 00:21:03,803
Where do you buy your bandanas?

492
00:21:04,643 --> 00:21:06,442
There are a lot of rare bandanas...

493
00:21:06,443 --> 00:21:08,183
- at rest stops on expressways.
- Right!

494
00:21:08,783 --> 00:21:10,683
- Right. I've seen them there.
- Aren't they good?

495
00:21:10,883 --> 00:21:13,152
- It's on the head of a mannequin.
- Something like that.

496
00:21:13,153 --> 00:21:15,022
- Right.
- Trot albums are right next to it.

497
00:21:15,023 --> 00:21:16,082
- Right.
- Yes.

498
00:21:16,083 --> 00:21:17,282
You bought them there?

499
00:21:17,283 --> 00:21:18,353
Yes, I did.

500
00:21:18,623 --> 00:21:22,092
No way! I thought you bought them
at a hip store in Hongdae.

501
00:21:22,093 --> 00:21:24,062
You might think they're all red.

502
00:21:24,063 --> 00:21:25,392
- Right.
- But they're all different.

503
00:21:25,393 --> 00:21:26,462
- Yes. I know.
- Right.

504
00:21:26,463 --> 00:21:28,202
Some of them have
drawings of mountains.

505
00:21:28,203 --> 00:21:29,632
- Right.
- Yes!

506
00:21:29,633 --> 00:21:31,203
They sell bandanas
at the foot of Mount Seorak.

507
00:21:31,273 --> 00:21:32,802
- Right. I buy them there too.
- Yes.

508
00:21:32,803 --> 00:21:33,833
That's right.

509
00:21:34,543 --> 00:21:36,773
(His bandana from a rest stop is
tightly fastened around his head.)

510
00:21:38,443 --> 00:21:41,683
(Looking stylish
with a gold earring)

511
00:21:42,943 --> 00:21:47,053
(With sunglasses,
he transforms into Captain Rock.)

512
00:21:48,223 --> 00:21:49,223
Great.

513
00:21:49,224 --> 00:21:50,953
(He sets out
after dressing up to the nines.)

514
00:21:51,823 --> 00:21:55,523
(Revving it up)

515
00:21:56,393 --> 00:21:59,263
(Captain Rock's red motorcycle
rolls down the road.)

516
00:22:00,533 --> 00:22:01,763
This is the Han Kyung Rok we know.

517
00:22:03,003 --> 00:22:04,573
He's so stylish.

518
00:22:06,203 --> 00:22:07,873
(As he stylishly rides
the motorcycle...)

519
00:22:08,673 --> 00:22:10,643
It's Hongdae. The smell of Seoul.

520
00:22:12,273 --> 00:22:14,373
I'm here.

521
00:22:17,643 --> 00:22:19,613
- It's a motorcycle shop.
- Yes.

522
00:22:19,653 --> 00:22:20,913
Is Sung Hwan at the shop?

523
00:22:21,983 --> 00:22:23,123
Hello.

524
00:22:23,923 --> 00:22:26,453
I want to check the tire pressure.

525
00:22:26,553 --> 00:22:28,092
Sometimes,

526
00:22:28,093 --> 00:22:30,192
I can't start the engine in one go.

527
00:22:30,193 --> 00:22:32,362
- Okay. Sure.
- Can you check on that too?

528
00:22:32,363 --> 00:22:33,633
Thank you. I love you.

529
00:22:33,763 --> 00:22:35,863
- Happy New Year.
- Happy New Year.

530
00:22:36,633 --> 00:22:38,002
(Excited)

531
00:22:38,003 --> 00:22:39,173
He's so cute.

532
00:22:40,203 --> 00:22:43,743
(He trots away after leaving
his motorcycle in the shop.)

533
00:22:44,913 --> 00:22:46,012
The sun feels amazing.

534
00:22:46,013 --> 00:22:47,673
(His mood is improved.)

535
00:22:49,013 --> 00:22:50,882
(He casually walks
into the streets of Hongdae.)

536
00:22:50,883 --> 00:22:52,812
- I haven't been there in ages.
- It's been so long for me too.

537
00:22:52,813 --> 00:22:53,953
The perfect street for a stroll.

538
00:22:54,213 --> 00:22:55,682
A lot has changed.

539
00:22:55,683 --> 00:22:56,853
- A lot has changed.
- Right.

540
00:22:57,623 --> 00:23:00,093
Hongdae is the hometown of my heart.

541
00:23:00,593 --> 00:23:03,523
The time I spent in Hongdae
making music...

542
00:23:04,063 --> 00:23:06,563
is longer than the time
I lived with my family.

543
00:23:06,633 --> 00:23:07,733
It's much longer.

544
00:23:07,793 --> 00:23:09,262
I go to Hongdae
more than three days a week...

545
00:23:09,263 --> 00:23:10,303
to practice and perform at shows.

546
00:23:10,763 --> 00:23:12,373
Even though
I don't spend a long time there,

547
00:23:12,673 --> 00:23:13,903
the moment I arrive in Hongdae,

548
00:23:14,273 --> 00:23:16,802
I'd tell myself,
"Hongdae even smells different."

549
00:23:16,803 --> 00:23:18,573
I get this nostalgic vibe
when I visit.

550
00:23:19,043 --> 00:23:23,183
A lot of my friends live
in every alley of Hongdae.

551
00:23:23,513 --> 00:23:25,583
I can't help but love Hongdae.

552
00:23:27,113 --> 00:23:28,783
I feel like
Hongdae has the best Seoul vibe.

553
00:23:28,953 --> 00:23:30,222
I had something like...

554
00:23:30,223 --> 00:23:32,122
longing for Hongdae.

555
00:23:32,123 --> 00:23:33,123
- Of course.
- So,

556
00:23:33,124 --> 00:23:36,063
when my friends and I reunited
after school break,

557
00:23:36,323 --> 00:23:37,422
we asked each other,
"What did you do during the break?"

558
00:23:37,423 --> 00:23:39,062
"I hung out in Hongdae."

559
00:23:39,063 --> 00:23:41,133
It was something to brag about.

560
00:23:41,663 --> 00:23:43,932
Yes. Hongdae represented Seoul.

561
00:23:43,933 --> 00:23:45,202
A visit to Hongdae made you stylish.

562
00:23:45,203 --> 00:23:46,403
- Yes.
- That's right.

563
00:23:46,533 --> 00:23:47,872
- Among friends in your hometown?
- Yes.

564
00:23:47,873 --> 00:23:50,702
I went to Hongdae
and ate Jopok Tteokbokki.

565
00:23:50,703 --> 00:23:52,412
- Jopok Tteokbokki.
- Jopok Tteokbokki.

566
00:23:52,413 --> 00:23:53,912
- It's a must. There are branches.
- It's a part of the course.

567
00:23:53,913 --> 00:23:54,913
- That's right.
- Ji Hyeon,

568
00:23:54,914 --> 00:23:56,382
I should give you a Hongdae tour.

569
00:23:56,383 --> 00:23:58,683
I should take you
to the nooks and crannies.

570
00:23:58,753 --> 00:24:00,012
Let's be his guides.

571
00:24:00,013 --> 00:24:01,683
Let's have a Hongdae tour.

572
00:24:05,053 --> 00:24:06,552
- Hello.
- Hello.

573
00:24:06,553 --> 00:24:08,593
- Hello.
- Happy New Year.

574
00:24:08,923 --> 00:24:10,163
- Happy New Year.
- Hello.

575
00:24:11,493 --> 00:24:13,363
Which coffee beans are good today?

576
00:24:13,663 --> 00:24:16,163
The ones from Rwanda are good.
The ones from Panama are unique.

577
00:24:16,503 --> 00:24:17,702
- Panama?
- Yes.

578
00:24:17,703 --> 00:24:19,073
- I'll buy a pack from Panama.
- Okay.

579
00:24:19,173 --> 00:24:21,743
And recommend me something
that's nutty without acidity.

580
00:24:21,943 --> 00:24:24,373
Among these options, I recommend
the ones from Nicaragua.

581
00:24:25,113 --> 00:24:26,173
Nicara...

582
00:24:26,743 --> 00:24:27,843
Hey, it's Nicara.

583
00:24:28,243 --> 00:24:29,313
I'm sorry.

584
00:24:30,113 --> 00:24:32,382
(Very funny)

585
00:24:32,383 --> 00:24:33,752
- That tells us his age.
- Yes.

586
00:24:33,753 --> 00:24:36,283
I also need a recommendation
on coffee I'll drink here.

587
00:24:36,383 --> 00:24:38,053
I'll just have a hot Americano.

588
00:24:38,223 --> 00:24:39,823
Do you want it to be acidic
as always?

589
00:24:39,993 --> 00:24:41,662
- I want it to be acidic.
- Okay.

590
00:24:41,663 --> 00:24:45,562
Did you enjoy
the Crying Nut concert?

591
00:24:45,563 --> 00:24:48,132
My voice was gone the next day.

592
00:24:48,133 --> 00:24:50,103
- From screaming?
- Everyone partied together.

593
00:24:50,403 --> 00:24:52,303
- I'll show you to the inside.
- The inside.

594
00:24:53,933 --> 00:24:57,543
(Rummaging)

595
00:24:58,143 --> 00:24:59,273
- Sir.
- Yes?

596
00:24:59,513 --> 00:25:00,772
It's a merchandise...

597
00:25:00,773 --> 00:25:02,043
- for Crying Nut's 30th anniversary.
- Okay.

598
00:25:02,183 --> 00:25:04,513
Take it as a souvenir. It's a gift.

599
00:25:04,643 --> 00:25:06,453
- Thank you.
- I always enjoy your coffee.

600
00:25:07,083 --> 00:25:08,083
Thank you.

601
00:25:08,084 --> 00:25:09,253
- You should come sometime.
- Thirtieth anniversary.

602
00:25:09,453 --> 00:25:12,193
A musician who can welcome
his 30th anniversary.

603
00:25:12,593 --> 00:25:14,993
(Proud)

604
00:25:15,123 --> 00:25:16,693
- Enjoy your coffee.
- Thank you.

605
00:25:19,133 --> 00:25:20,533
(Sipping)

606
00:25:21,263 --> 00:25:22,263
(My goodness.)

607
00:25:27,433 --> 00:25:28,543
It thaws my body.

608
00:25:30,173 --> 00:25:31,742
- Bye.
- Thank you.

609
00:25:31,743 --> 00:25:33,913
- Happy New Year.
- Happy New Year.

610
00:25:35,313 --> 00:25:36,712
It's the clock tower.

611
00:25:36,713 --> 00:25:38,153
(Happy)

612
00:25:40,083 --> 00:25:42,623
(Singing)

613
00:25:43,753 --> 00:25:45,292
(He enters a building as he hums.)

614
00:25:45,293 --> 00:25:46,752
- Is it still there?
- That's right.

615
00:25:46,753 --> 00:25:48,363
(He enters a building as he hums.)

616
00:25:49,723 --> 00:25:51,863
- Hello.
- Hello.

617
00:25:52,463 --> 00:25:54,263
- Just today,
- Okay.

618
00:25:55,203 --> 00:25:56,803
- my flowers wilted.
- Right.

619
00:25:57,333 --> 00:25:59,273
- I came to get new flowers.
- Did he go to a flower shop?

620
00:26:00,233 --> 00:26:03,543
(He's a flower enthusiast who enjoys
his therapeutic time with flowers.)

621
00:26:04,273 --> 00:26:06,442
- I...
- The flowers you want...

622
00:26:06,443 --> 00:26:08,383
- Okay.
- are over here.

623
00:26:10,143 --> 00:26:12,153
(Full of pretty flowers
of various shapes and colors)

624
00:26:13,013 --> 00:26:14,953
Seriously, nothing is going
as I expected today.

625
00:26:15,983 --> 00:26:17,722
It's like a fairy's day.

626
00:26:17,723 --> 00:26:18,852
A fairy's day.

627
00:26:18,853 --> 00:26:21,893
I think this one suits you well.

628
00:26:22,023 --> 00:26:24,223
Gerbera is a nice choice too.

629
00:26:24,623 --> 00:26:25,762
- As for gerbera,
- Okay.

630
00:26:25,763 --> 00:26:28,303
I recently received it as a gift.

631
00:26:29,063 --> 00:26:30,962
- It'll look nice with yellow roses.
- Okay.

632
00:26:30,963 --> 00:26:32,903
- Yellow roses.
- He knows all the flowers.

633
00:26:33,533 --> 00:26:35,202
Those purple flowers are nice too.

634
00:26:35,203 --> 00:26:36,343
Yes. They're pretty.

635
00:26:36,703 --> 00:26:38,442
- Complementary colors...
- Isn't this a common poppy?

636
00:26:38,443 --> 00:26:39,542
It's an anemone.

637
00:26:39,543 --> 00:26:40,673
- Anemone.
- Yes.

638
00:26:41,443 --> 00:26:43,282
Don't anemones represent
one-sided love?

639
00:26:43,283 --> 00:26:45,253
- "Even if you don't love me,"
- That's right.

640
00:26:45,413 --> 00:26:47,752
- "I'll love you."
- That's right.

641
00:26:47,753 --> 00:26:48,982
Do you know
the meaning of each flower?

642
00:26:48,983 --> 00:26:50,252
I know it for a few flowers.

643
00:26:50,253 --> 00:26:51,593
- It's perfect.
- Yes.

644
00:26:52,123 --> 00:26:53,193
It's very pretty.

645
00:26:54,623 --> 00:26:55,693
It's cold, so...

646
00:26:56,563 --> 00:26:58,092
- Do you carry it in your coat?
- Let's go like this.

647
00:26:58,093 --> 00:26:59,333
It's cold outside.

648
00:27:00,293 --> 00:27:02,262
Bye. All right.

649
00:27:02,263 --> 00:27:03,403
(He puts the flowers in his coat
so that they wouldn't be cold.)

650
00:27:03,903 --> 00:27:06,432
It's cold.

651
00:27:06,433 --> 00:27:09,343
(He navigates the streets of Hongdae
with ease.)

652
00:27:10,243 --> 00:27:11,672
The streets are slippery.

653
00:27:11,673 --> 00:27:13,913
(He walks diligently.)

654
00:27:17,583 --> 00:27:18,583
Oh, my.

655
00:27:18,584 --> 00:27:19,812
(He enters a building
in the alleys.)

656
00:27:19,813 --> 00:27:20,983
What's this place?

657
00:27:22,053 --> 00:27:23,222
Is anyone here?

658
00:27:23,223 --> 00:27:24,223
(Hey!)

659
00:27:25,293 --> 00:27:27,453
(Full of rock spirit)

660
00:27:28,823 --> 00:27:30,622
(A rock-concept store
with charismatic mood)

661
00:27:30,623 --> 00:27:33,492
I thought
your house would look like that.

662
00:27:33,493 --> 00:27:34,662
- Seriously.
- That's right.

663
00:27:34,663 --> 00:27:35,862
- I mean...
- You're full of prejudices.

664
00:27:35,863 --> 00:27:36,932
That's right.

665
00:27:36,933 --> 00:27:39,902
Ji Hyeon, it's like expecting
your place to be full of spangles.

666
00:27:39,903 --> 00:27:41,003
That's right.

667
00:27:41,073 --> 00:27:42,503
Is that it?

668
00:27:43,003 --> 00:27:44,342
- Happy New Year.
- Happy New Year.

669
00:27:44,343 --> 00:27:46,113
Hey, why do you have flowers
on your chest?

670
00:27:46,273 --> 00:27:48,143
I thought they might be cold.

671
00:27:48,483 --> 00:27:49,713
- The flowers?
- Yes.

672
00:27:50,213 --> 00:27:52,952
I bought a small gift...

673
00:27:52,953 --> 00:27:54,383
because we haven't seen each other
in a while.

674
00:27:55,583 --> 00:27:56,653
My goodness.

675
00:27:57,383 --> 00:27:58,952
- Tiger.
- You shouldn't have.

676
00:27:58,953 --> 00:28:01,092
Hey, Crying Nut welcomed...

677
00:28:01,093 --> 00:28:02,623
- its 30th anniversary.
- Okay.

678
00:28:02,923 --> 00:28:04,662
- Did you even write a letter?
- Yes.

679
00:28:04,663 --> 00:28:06,462
You're close to all the owners
in the neighborhood.

680
00:28:06,463 --> 00:28:09,133
They're my friends
in the neighborhood.

681
00:28:09,733 --> 00:28:11,333
Does Street Guns have any plans...

682
00:28:11,673 --> 00:28:13,503
for a concert or an album?

683
00:28:13,703 --> 00:28:14,942
We're still recording.

684
00:28:14,943 --> 00:28:16,572
- Are you still recording?
- Yes.

685
00:28:16,573 --> 00:28:18,342
- Are Cheol Su and all doing well?
- Cheol Su is getting married.

686
00:28:18,343 --> 00:28:19,373
What?

687
00:28:20,243 --> 00:28:21,982
- With whom?
- Dorothy.

688
00:28:21,983 --> 00:28:23,042
- Dorothy?
- Yes.

689
00:28:23,043 --> 00:28:25,112
- Dorothy?
- How nice. They'll get married.

690
00:28:25,113 --> 00:28:26,282
- Those names...
- Can Cheol Su and Dorothy...

691
00:28:26,283 --> 00:28:27,512
even get married?

692
00:28:27,513 --> 00:28:28,552
- It's very...
- There are...

693
00:28:28,553 --> 00:28:29,952
many international marriages.

694
00:28:29,953 --> 00:28:32,392
In Hongdae, people use stage names.

695
00:28:32,393 --> 00:28:34,492
(Please don't misunderstand.)

696
00:28:34,493 --> 00:28:36,162
- It was nice seeing you.
- Keep on rocking.

697
00:28:36,163 --> 00:28:37,463
We'll keep on rocking, for sure.

698
00:28:38,563 --> 00:28:41,293
(He hits the streets again
after the third visit.)

699
00:28:41,803 --> 00:28:44,063
Kyung Rok, you live
the life of a president.

700
00:28:44,163 --> 00:28:45,202
He's the president of Hongdae.

701
00:28:45,203 --> 00:28:47,573
I made a round
to see the faces I had missed.

702
00:28:47,873 --> 00:28:49,773
How many numbers do you have
in your contact?

703
00:28:50,343 --> 00:28:52,043
I think I have over 3,000.

704
00:28:52,373 --> 00:28:53,542
Did you say 3,000?

705
00:28:53,543 --> 00:28:56,283
Yes. My friends jokingly tell me...

706
00:28:56,483 --> 00:28:58,483
to run for Mapo-gu's
district representative.

707
00:28:58,813 --> 00:29:00,312
- You'll get the position.
- Seriously.

708
00:29:00,313 --> 00:29:02,223
- You'll get many votes.
- Yes.

709
00:29:04,283 --> 00:29:07,022
(Hongdae President continues
to make a round with ease.)

710
00:29:07,023 --> 00:29:08,052
(What's this place?)

711
00:29:08,053 --> 00:29:09,063
Hey.

712
00:29:10,363 --> 00:29:11,832
- What's up?
- He's so popular.

713
00:29:11,833 --> 00:29:12,993
- Happy New Year.
- Happy New Year.

714
00:29:13,233 --> 00:29:14,593
- Merry Christmas.
- Merry Christmas.

715
00:29:15,233 --> 00:29:16,302
(Jebi Coffee House)

716
00:29:16,303 --> 00:29:17,962
- It's Jebi Coffee House.
- Jebi Coffee House.

717
00:29:17,963 --> 00:29:19,403
(When you enter
through the red iron door)

718
00:29:20,633 --> 00:29:21,773
This is a place...

719
00:29:22,273 --> 00:29:25,372
everyone in Hongdae drops by.

720
00:29:25,373 --> 00:29:26,773
That's right.

721
00:29:28,583 --> 00:29:30,713
It's a fun place
because the ceiling is open.

722
00:29:31,043 --> 00:29:33,313
You can watch the performance
from the upper floor.

723
00:29:33,813 --> 00:29:35,923
- Everyone looks down.
- That's right.

724
00:29:36,883 --> 00:29:39,322
Jebi Coffee House is a place...

725
00:29:39,323 --> 00:29:42,662
where the audience members
and artists who want to have...

726
00:29:42,663 --> 00:29:44,792
their unique colors gather around.

727
00:29:44,793 --> 00:29:46,563
It's like a hideout.

728
00:29:47,093 --> 00:29:49,533
(The lineup of the performances
is written at the entrance.)

729
00:29:50,133 --> 00:29:52,072
(IU, HYUKOH)

730
00:29:52,073 --> 00:29:53,472
(A hideout that's visited
by various artists)

731
00:29:53,473 --> 00:29:55,443
(Car the garden)

732
00:29:55,873 --> 00:29:59,313
(Chang Ki Ha)

733
00:29:59,873 --> 00:30:02,513
(The artists enjoy themselves
with the audience members here.)

734
00:30:02,613 --> 00:30:05,553
(Still, isn't that good?)

735
00:30:06,353 --> 00:30:07,413
What's this?

736
00:30:08,353 --> 00:30:11,023
People who make an effort
to preserve...

737
00:30:11,453 --> 00:30:13,152
the unique culture of Hongdae...

738
00:30:13,153 --> 00:30:15,963
gather around in this place.

739
00:30:16,293 --> 00:30:18,263
It's such a meaningful place.

740
00:30:19,563 --> 00:30:20,793
What? Is that Ryoo Seung Bum?

741
00:30:21,563 --> 00:30:24,933
He's a member
of Bulnabang Star Sausage Club,

742
00:30:25,603 --> 00:30:27,903
- Cho Carlos.
- Bulnabang Star Sausage Club.

743
00:30:28,133 --> 00:30:29,903
How do they come up
with these names?

744
00:30:30,143 --> 00:30:32,373
These days, there's a band called
Cha Cha with the Cousins.

745
00:30:32,673 --> 00:30:34,742
- That's right.
- Are they doing well?

746
00:30:34,743 --> 00:30:37,212
Back in the day, they had
an Arabic-sounding name.

747
00:30:37,213 --> 00:30:39,582
- Sultan of the Disco.
- Sultan of the Disco.

748
00:30:39,583 --> 00:30:40,953
Where are they?

749
00:30:41,313 --> 00:30:42,482
- As for them...
- They...

750
00:30:42,483 --> 00:30:44,582
- One member of Nahzam Sue.
- Sultan of the Disco.

751
00:30:44,583 --> 00:30:45,792
- Nahzam Sue?
- So...

752
00:30:45,793 --> 00:30:48,223
Hongdae is a chaotic place.

753
00:30:48,323 --> 00:30:51,222
It isn't anything bad.
So you need a wild name.

754
00:30:51,223 --> 00:30:53,462
- I see.
- You should turn your weakness...

755
00:30:53,463 --> 00:30:54,933
into your strength.

756
00:30:55,563 --> 00:30:56,763
- How hip.
- Nice.

757
00:30:57,203 --> 00:30:58,773
Hair Fibers Moomoo Fifty.

758
00:30:59,203 --> 00:31:00,203
- Something like that.
- A band name?

759
00:31:00,204 --> 00:31:01,502
- Hair Fibers Moomoo...
- Yes.

